children.text:
The specification consists of three main parts: its guidelines, its base and its technical appendix. Each part of the specification answers a question of its own:

- the guidelines say how the specification is kept and implemented;
- the base specification says what the system does;
- the technical appendix says how the system is built.

Where needed, the base specification may be divided into sections. If it is divided, the technical appendix inherits the structure of the base specification where the two overlap.

Everything that bears on a node is kept in that node:

- its reason;
- its links;
- the decisions taken on it;
- its todos;
- the comments on it.

None of it is kept in a separate document.

The wording an approval covers is a node's kind, title, text, reason and examples, and the fields the node's kind adds alongside them, such as `TextRule.list`. A change to any of these makes the node a draft, even where the change is made by one of the node's owners. The wording of a node in a cell of a `TableRule` also includes the text cells of its row and the headings of its column and of its group.

An approval survives a change to:
- the node's links or its metadata;
- who its owners are;
- what is built into it from other nodes;
- where it is in the specification.

An agent working for one of a node's owners approves the node on that owner's behalf in each of the following cases, and only in those:

- the node's wording changes only to state what one of its owners approved in another node;
- the node's wording changes only through a replacement that does not change its meaning, such as a renamed term or path;
- the node belongs to the technical appendix (§s-qmmzf9nksfsa3qp), and all of the following hold:
  - the agent wrote the node while implementing a rule of the base specification;
  - the node has an `implements` link (§s-sn6au3vcr9uy5pm) to that rule;
  - that rule is approved.

The agent reports each of these approvals to the owner on whose behalf it was made.

The system's code (§s-hheus6f395ynqvc) other than tests is bound by SpecTags (§s-8xw4mgvpexyvw5q) to the rules it implements. Code that implements no rule has a SpecTag that says so, and that SpecTag binds the code too.

A symbol is a named unit of code, such as a function, a class or a member of one, a type, a constant or a component. Whoever writes code binds it to its rules in one of the following ways:
- by a SpecTag directly before each symbol, if the checks (§s-jau82aq9juv9zzh) can identify the file's symbols;
- by a SpecTag in each symbol's comment property, such as `$comment` in JSON Schema, for a file whose format allows no comments but gives each of its symbols such a property, if the checks can identify the file's symbols;
- by SpecTags in a comment placed as near the start of the file as its format allows, if the checks cannot identify the file's symbols;
- by an entry in the bindings file at the root of the repository, for any other file whose format allows no comments.

In a file whose symbols the checks can identify, the code that is outside every symbol and is not an import is bound by SpecTags in a comment placed as near the start of the file as its format allows or, where the format allows no comments, in the comment property of the file's top-level object.

A SpecTag before a symbol, or in its comment property, binds that symbol and every symbol nested in it. SpecTags at the start of a file whose symbols the checks cannot identify, and an entry in the bindings file, bind the whole file. A SpecTag that names a node that is not a rule, such as a section, binds nothing. A change anywhere in a file bound as a whole is a change to the code its SpecTags bind. The only exception is an edit that changes no code (§s-9j2mmnuuwmx5ksn): an edit to a comment that leaves its SpecTags as they are, or to whitespace that does not affect what the code does.

The formats that allow no comments are JSON and those that a project's own guidelines (§s-u7tw6m8npxmv5tu) name as allowing none.

Each entry in the bindings file names an existing file and holds that file's SpecTags.

In the review of a commit, an independent reviewer judges the following (§s-nyh9yk22ba6g77q):
- for every commit that changes the system's code (§s-hheus6f395ynqvc) outside the files generated from the specification (§s-qyjw8xez6t6adc3): whether that code does what the rules named in the commit say and does nothing that they do not say;
- for every commit that adds or changes a comment in the code or in a test outside the files generated from the specification: whether each such comment is consistent with the code that it describes and with the rules that the code implements or that the test is written from;
- for a commit whose message states that a rule's implementation needs no change: whether that statement is true;
- for a commit in which a node of the technical appendix (§s-qmmzf9nksfsa3qp) is approved, if the node has an `implements` link (§s-sn6au3vcr9uy5pm) to a rule of the base specification (§s-5x737t9nee4zgaa): whether the node refines the rules of the base specification that it implements and contradicts no rule of the base specification.

A rule of an extension of the guidelines (§s-mbmnsnxenmavu3z) relies only on the SpecSheriff guidelines and on the extension it belongs to:
- of the words that take their meaning from the specification (§s-qjkx3zs8r8kj69t), its text and its reason use only those that the guidelines or that extension define;
- its text and its reason cite only nodes of the guidelines or of that extension;
- the links that the rule declares are only of the types that the guidelines or that extension define (§s-sn6au3vcr9uy5pm), and lead only to nodes of the guidelines or of that extension.

The guidelines consist of the rules for keeping the specification and implementing it. They state what is stored, what is checked, when, and what a failure costs.

How one toolset carries out these rules is described in a separate document, the appendix of the tools. It is not part of the guidelines. It describes:

- the toolset's commands;
- its hooks;
- where it keeps the files;
- how it reads code;
- who runs its reviewer.

The guidelines of a project are:

- the SpecSheriff guidelines, made up of the rules every project shares;
- the extensions of the guidelines that the project has chosen (§s-mbmnsnxenmavu3z);
- the project's own guidelines, which set out what only its specification needs, such as who owns the specification.

Beyond what the SpecSheriff guidelines and the chosen extensions require of a project's own guidelines, the project may add rules of its own where it needs them.

Each rule of a project's own guidelines refines or extends a rule of the SpecSheriff guidelines or of a chosen extension. It never contradicts that rule.

The technical appendix contains:

- a section for each top-level section of the base specification it implements, under that section's title;
- a section for the stack;
- technical debt (§s-2r7k2aeyusgy6fw).

For a user interface, the base specification states what a user can rely on in any instance of the system:

- what is shown;
- what it means;
- in what order;
- in which states;
- what the user can do.

How it looks and is built is described in the technical appendix: placement, size, colour, icon, the kind of control, where a state is kept.

A node declares a link to every other node it cites. The link is kept in the node's `refs` list, apart from the citation. A node cites another node when any of its properties contains `§` followed by that node's sid, even in text formatted as code. The following properties are ignored:
- the node's links;
- the nodes under it;
- values built from other nodes.

A node of the archive is not held to this rule.

Whoever writes a node chooses its wording so that whoever applies the node understands it correctly on first reading and with the least effort. The other rules of this section serve this goal. Where two of them conflict, the writer chooses the wording that serves the goal better.

The reader of a node is whoever applies it:
- for a node that describes the system, whoever writes tests and code from the node;
- for a node that governs how the specification is kept and implemented, whoever writes the specification, the tests or the code in accordance with the node.

Whoever writes a node assumes that its reader:
- learns what the node means only from the specification;
- sees the whole node;
- can read the nodes that the node links to;
- knows the rest of the specification only in part.

Each of the other rules of this section applies to the parts of a node that it names. A rule that names none applies to the node's title, text, reason, list members and table cells, except the citations in them.

A node may use the following terms without explaining them, since every reader is given their definitions: node, sid, rule, title, text, reason, link, citation, section, specification, guidelines, owner, draft, approved.

A general word appears in the same phrase as what it applies to. So a rule that names a structure, a check or a model also says:
- which record the structure describes;
- when the check is made and what it compares with what;
- what the model represents.

Where a rule refers to a specific thing, it uses the name of that thing.

This rule applies to a node's text, reason, list members and table cells.

A name is the word or phrase by which the specification refers to an entity on its own. An entity is anything that the specification defines, for example:
- a concept from the subject area, such as a user, a record or a request;
- a procedure or a constant;
- a concept from the guidelines, such as a node or a link;
- a property of another entity, such as a field of a record.

A name means the same thing in every node that uses it.

The table gives the principles that a name follows. Its last two columns hold examples, which state no requirement.

Where a rule restricts something by a list, it says whether the list names what is allowed or what is forbidden. It chooses according to which mistake would go unnoticed:
- where something left out would go unnoticed, the list names what is forbidden, as with the records a backup leaves out;
- where something let in would go unnoticed, the list names what is allowed, as with the fields a public API returns.

Each sentence of a node states one thought: a statement that can be agreed with or disputed on its own.

A sentence that states one relation between two facts states one thought, even though each fact could be disputed on its own. Examples of such a relation:
- a cause and its effect: "X, so Y";
- a contrast: "X, but Y";
- a condition: "if X, Y".

A sentence that sets two facts side by side with no relation between them, or that states two relations, states two thoughts.

A rule describes the system or governs how the specification is kept and implemented. Whoever writes or revises a rule removes it where it:

- does neither;
- forbids what no rule requires;
- states the obvious.

Each sentence of a node is as short as it can be while keeping all of its meaning, correct grammar and natural wording. Whoever shortens a sentence keeps every condition and every exception in it. They never:
- leave out its subject or its verb;
- replace a verb with a string of nouns;
- leave out an article, a preposition or any other function word that a careful native writer of English would use;
- replace words with a label or an abbreviation that the specification does not define and a reader might not know.

Whoever writes a rule makes it a `TableRule` only where at least one of these holds:
- the values in a column are to be checked against those that the code declares;
- the rule's text requires something of every row;
- a requirement in a cell needs to be a node of its own.

Elsewhere, a table is written in the node's text.

Each of these nodes is considered a rule of a section, for the purposes of the requirement that the section contains at least one rule:

- a term the section contains;
- a node of a project's own kind that the section contains;
- a rule of a section nested in that section.

A node's location names where it is in the specification: the part it is in, followed by its place there. A node's place is its number among the nodes under the same parent. The following nodes take no number:
- a term (§s-khqsmy6mhejkrhg), whose place is the term's name;
- the first top-level node of a file of the guidelines, of the appendix of the tools (§s-a4rbry4hsu34hy8) or of the technical appendix (§s-tj38pvm38nepaf5), which takes as its location the part's name followed by the file's name;
- a node of a kind that an extension of the guidelines (§s-mbmnsnxenmavu3z) defines and that a folder of the base specification (§s-5x737t9nee4zgaa) opens with, which takes the folder's name alone as its location.

In each file of the guidelines, of the appendix of the tools or of the technical appendix, the nodes are numbered under the location of the file's first top-level node. The nodes under a node whose location is the folder's name alone are numbered together with the other top-level nodes of the folder.

The location is computed from where the node is and is never written by hand.

If the specification's approval status is `approved`, the system's code must implement every approved rule. Such a rule is considered implemented when both of the following hold (§s-8xw4mgvpexyvw5q):
- code of the system other than tests is bound to it (§s-kgbzk2ph9e6jfb4);
- a test bound to it passes.

Code or a test bound to a node that has an `implements` link (§s-sn6au3vcr9uy5pm) to such a rule, directly or through a chain of such links, counts as bound to that rule.

The requirement that a rule be implemented does not apply to:
- the rules of the SpecSheriff guidelines and of the extensions that the project has chosen (§s-9ttvcwpqkehm58g);
- the rules of the project's own guidelines;
- a rule of a project's own kind of node whose nodes require no implementation in code (§s-vsh384ybt4w25a2);
- the descriptions of shortcomings in the technical debt (§s-2r7k2aeyusgy6fw);
- a rule exempted from implementation (§s-gf6n8ge4mke3pr2).

A change to a SpecTag (§s-8xw4mgvpexyvw5q) is considered a change to the test or the code that the SpecTag binds.

As long as the SpecTag stays the same, none of the following changes the test or the code:
- renaming a test;
- adding, removing or rewording a comment;
- changing whitespace that, in the language of its file, does not affect what the code does.

In a file whose comments and whitespace the checks (§s-jau82aq9juv9zzh) cannot tell apart from its code, every edit changes the code.

The checks treat code that is outside the commit, and that no SpecTag (§s-8xw4mgvpexyvw5q) binds, as follows:
- while the specification is a draft, the commit passes without a warning;
- while the specification is approved and its `strict` flag (§s-q3pfzyaxvpr2zj2) is off, the commit passes, and a single warning for all such code says that such code exists;
- while the specification is approved and its `strict` flag is on, the commit is refused.

Code outside the commit means the system's code that the commit leaves unchanged, tests included. The warning in the second case and the refusal in the third each name the command of the SpecSheriff tools that lists that code by file and symbol.

node.text:
Every node of the specification is approved only while an approval of its current wording is in force. The check leaves out the following nodes:
- the nodes of the SpecSheriff guidelines that a project uses and of the extensions that the project has chosen (§s-9ttvcwpqkehm58g);
- sections and any other nodes whose approval status (§s-tgrnpagrb58tya7) is derived from other nodes

An approved rule that the system's code (§s-hheus6f395ynqvc) must implement (§s-6fw72j65jkqrfpf) is bound both to code of the system other than tests and to a test that is not marked as skipped or as expected to fail. Code or a test bound to a node that has an `implements` link (§s-sn6au3vcr9uy5pm) to the approved rule, directly or through a chain of such links, also counts as bound to the approved rule.

The check applies only to the approved rules that a commit includes. A commit includes an approved rule if any of the following happens in that commit:
- the rule's approval is added;
- a test or code that is bound to the rule, before the commit or after it, changes;
- an `implements` link through which a test or code is bound to the rule is removed;
- the rule stops falling under the exceptions to the requirement that a rule be implemented (§s-6fw72j65jkqrfpf), for example because its exemption from implementation ends (§s-gf6n8ge4mke3pr2) or because it is moved out of the technical debt section (§s-2r7k2aeyusgy6fw).

